--- COURSE OUTLINE AS CIRCULATED ---

BITS SCHOOL OF MANAGEMENT
                              Term 4, Block 19, 2025 - 26
                                            Course Outline

Course Title: Operations Strategy

Faculty: Professor Dr. Santiago Kraiselburd

Dates: June 30th to July 13th, 2025

Contact details: skraiselburd@tec.mx

Academic Associate: Mohd. Juned

Contact details: mohd.juned@bitsom.edu.in

Office Hours: By prior appointment only

Course Description: Operations Strategy in Emerging Markets is aimed at broadening your understanding of
operating challenges well beyond topics covered in core courses, and of specific challenges that arise when
operating in the global south. Looking at businesses ranging from heavy manufacturing to biotechnology, and
covering many of the main industries in the region, the course focuses on the value created and captured by
different operating strategies.

In particular, the course will cover the following main topics:
Part I: The Concept of an Operations Strategy. This module provides an overview of key diagnostic and
analytical tools for identifying, framing, and solving strategic operating issues. The idea is to go beyond basic
industry analysis, assessing a company's operations strategy, and how it fits the specific environment where it
must operate.
Part II: Value chain design, planning and execution under uncertainty. This section discusses three key concepts
to deal with uncertainty, which is especially prevalent in the region: agility to enable faster reaction to what
cannot be anticipated, planning to allow for preparation of what can be anticipated, and establishment of early
warning systems to detect and react to events in real time, ranging from minor disruptions to worldwide events
such as the current pandemic.
Part III: Industry 4.0, with a focus on Agribusiness and Biotech. This section introduced the idea of the
(coming?) fourth industrial revolution, focusing on Agribusiness and Biotech, that are at the heart of the action in
many emerging markets. We will also talk about the biotech start up scene in the global south, and the unique
challenges that it faces.

Important: this course follows the case method of teaching for most sessions. As you may know, the case method
of teaching is based on the idea that you, as a group, will discover the issues and learn from the ideas of others in
the class. There will be only a limited number of lectures, and, therefore, it is essential that you come to class
prepared to share your insights and analysis with others. Since the course´s schedule is intense, there will be just

enough time to read all the assigned materials during the week of class when we will meet. Therefore, it is
important that you assign enough preparation time during the course´s week.

Methodology
To achieve the course objectives most effectively, you will need to:
a) be prepared and discuss the case problems in class (very important);
b) work in teams to discuss cases;
c) attend presentations and classes and be ready to make comments/respond to questions.
c) take and pass the final exam (a case analysis)

Course Materials:
Mentioned session-wise.

Assessment: Maximum Marks 100

    1. Class Participation 20%
    2. Group Assignment 40%
    3. Final Exam 40%

Class Participation
The case method of teaching is based on the idea that you, as a group, will discover the issues and learn from the
ideas of others in the class. There will be no lectures, and, therefore, it is essential that you come to class
prepared to share your insights and analysis with others. Students will be evaluated on the quality (not the
quantity) of the contributions. The criteria used to judge effective participation includes:

1.      Is the participant a good listener and open to evaluating opposing perspectives in a constructive manner?
2.      Are the points made relevant to the current discussion? Are they linked to the comments of others?
3.      Do the comments demonstrate appropriate and insightful analysis of case data? Do they clarify earlier
ideas and present a clearer statement of the select concepts and issues?
4.      Did the participant integrate concepts from prior classes and assigned readings into current discussion
where possible?

Group Assignment
The group assignment grade will be the results of the simulation in Sessions 9&10 of Day 5. On that day, after
playing we will discuss the simulations results and goals, debriefing the results.
The group size will be 4-5. Students get to pick their groups.

Final exam
Open notes, take home exam, delivered online. The exam case will be available on the course´s online platform.
You are expected to produce one analysis/report the exam case, following the assigned questions as guidance.
The analysis should not exceed 2,500 words in text and should not include more than six exhibits. Just as in class
participation I will be looking for quality and not quantity.

The following criteria will be used in evaluating the assignment:
1.       Does the author address and analyze the major case issues?
2.       Does the analysis incorporate the relevant concepts/tools from course notes and assigned readings
properly?
3.       Are assumptions made in the analysis stated explicitly?
4.       Are the criteria for choosing alternative recommendations clearly sated? Are the criteria appropriate?
5.       Is the plan of action, when called for, integrated in a logical way and linked to the analysis? Is the action
plan specific, complete and practical?
6.       Do the exhibits support and add to the text on key points?
7.       Is the presentation of professional quality?
8.       Is the paper logically consistent and effectively structured? How convincing is the analysis? Is it likely
that the recommendations will achieve their intended results?

The reports are due on a time and date to be announced in class, and should be delivered via the course platform.
Late deliveries imply a penalty equal to 50% of the points you would otherwise get. You are not supposed to
discuss anything about the exam with other students, both analysis and writing are to be strictly individual. You
can consult the internet and books, as long as you follow standard anti plagiarism rules, but use/consultation of
“case solutions” or other people’s reports on the same case is strictly forbidden.

General comments
Except for the final exam, groupwork for case preparation and assignments is encouraged. As additional
feedback, please feel comfortable to make an appointment with me to sit down and discuss any problems you
encounter with the course.

POLICY:

Class Policies
   We will start classes on time.
   If you are late, the instructor has the choice of not allowing you to attend or creating creative punishments for
    tardiness.

Attendance Policy
  The attendance policy as laid out by the Programme Office applies. Please refer to your student handbook
  for details.

Honour Code
  BITSoM takes the honour code seriously, and so should you. The student handbook contains details on the
  honour code policy at BITSoM. You are expected to abide by an honour code and follow a culture of
  honesty. Please ensure that you read the relevant section(s) in the handbook. Feel free to ask us in case you
  have any questions.

