Session Schedule

                                     Part I
                      THE CONCEPT OF OPERATIONS STRATEGY

Day 1

SESSION 1. Overcoming industrial structural weaknesses through operations
Southwest Airlines in Baltimore (Product number: 602156-PDF-ENG)
This case presents comparative data to illuminate the key elements of Southwest's operating strategy
and provides detailed information about the activities and information flows required to turn around a
plane, allowing for a meaningful analysis of the process--e.g., resource utilization, capacity,
bottlenecks, and coordination mechanisms.

Be prepared to discuss the following questions in class. Draw on information in the case itself, other
readings, your relevant work experience, and any assumptions you may consider reasonable.

1. Why has the airline industry, historically, been marginally profitable on average (i.e., according to
IATA, it has an average profitability of approx. 2.5%)?
2. How does Southwest Airlines (SWA) compete? What advantages does it have over other airlines?
2. The boarding and disembarkation process requires coordination between 12 functional groups in
SWA to allow, in a short period of time, to serve an incoming aircraft, and load it with passengers and
bags for a prompt departure. Please analyze this process in Baltimore. How well does it work?
3. Why is operational performance in Baltimore declining? What issues do you identify as requiring
action?
4. What would you recommend Matt Harfner do?

Suggested Complementary Reading:
What Is Strategy?, Michael E. Porter, Harvard Business Review, 1 de febrero de 2000. (Product
Number: 4134)

Note on the Structural Analysis of Industries, Michael E. Porter, Harvard Business School. (Product
Number: 9-376-054)

SESSION 2 Overcoming industrial structural weaknesses through operations (Part II)
Nucor at a Crossroads (Product Number: 9-793-039)

Nucor is a minimill deciding whether to spend a significant fraction of its net worth on a commercially
unproven technology in order to penetrate a large but hitherto inaccessible segment of the steel market.

Be prepared to discuss the following questions in class. Draw on information in the case itself, other
readings, your relevant work experience, and any assumptions you may consider reasonable.

1. According to McKinsey, global steel industry EBITDA margins have averaged eight to ten percent,
    which is below the margin of 15 to 17 percent necessary for long-term sustainability. What
    pressures this industry´s average profitability downwards?
2. Why has Nucor performed so well in the past?
3. How attractive do the economics of thin-slab casting look?
3.      Is thin slab-casting likely to afford Nucor a sustainable competitive advantage in flat-rolled
products?
4.      How should Nucor think about the uncertainties surrounding thin-slab casting? What should it
do?

Suggested Complementary Reading:
The Art of Keeping Management Simple: An Interview with Ken Iverson of Nucor Steel, Tom
Brown; Ken Iverson, Harvard Management Update Article, May 01, 1998. Available at the end of the
class. (Product Number: U9805B).

Day 2.
SESSION 3 Market entry and operations-based strengths: the point of view of the entrant.

Market entry and operations as a competitive advantage

Australian Paper Manufacturers (A) (Product Number: 9-691-041)

Describes a company which has broken an unwritten cordial agreement amongst the three Australian
paper manufacturers to split the domestic market three ways by market segment.

Be prepared to discuss the following questions in class. Draw on information in the case itself, other
readings, your relevant work experience, and any assumptions you may consider reasonable.

1.      What opportunities and risks did Ken McRae face as he contemplated taking APM into the fine
papers market? Be specific with respect to technological, operations, and capital investment (as well as
other) considerations.
2.      How has the environment fared in this battle?
3.      As Ken McRae, what technology and operations strategy options are available? Which do you
think he ought to pursue? Why?

Note: the B case (Product Number: 9-691-043) will be available at the end of the class.

Suggested Complementary Reading:
Competing Through Manufacturing, Steven C. Wheelwright and Robert H. Hayes, Harvard Business
Review, January-February, 1985. (Product Number: 85117)

SESSION 4 Reacting to market entrants: the point of view of the incumbent.
American Connector Co. (A) (Product Number: 9-693-035)

American Connector Co. is forced to reexamine operations at its Sunnyvale plant when a Japanese
competitor announces plans to build an "ultimate" plant in the United States.

Be prepared to discuss the following questions in class. Draw on information in the case itself, other
readings, your relevant work experience, and any assumptions you may consider reasonable.

1.      Compare the manufacturing structures and strategies of DJC’s Kawasaki plant and ACC’s
Sunnyvale plant.
2.      What impact does each plant’s manufacturing strategy have on its ability to achieve different
competitive objectives, such as low cost, high reliability, flexibility, and product innovation?
3.      If DJC built a new plant in the U.S., what changes would you recommend in its manufacturing
structure as compared with the Kawasaki plant? What, for example, would be the impact on the
Sunnyvale plant if it doubled its average production run length? How serious a threat would ACC face
if DJC opened a new plant in the U.S.?
4.      What changes would you now recommend in the operations of ACC’s Sunnyvale plant?

Note: the B case (Product Number: 9-693-049) will be available at the end of the class.

Suggested Complementary Reading:
Postindustrial Manufacturing, Ramchandran Jaikumar, Harvard Business Review, November 01,
1986. (Product Number: 86606)
The Focused Factory, C. Wickham Skinner, Harvard Business Review, May 01, 1974. (Product
Number: 74308)

Day 3

SESSION 5 Operations and economic downturns.
Halloran Metals (Product Number: 9-683-062)

Two competitors in the U.S. Northeast steel service center industry have made very different choices
with regards to logistics and operating strategy.

Be prepared to discuss the following questions in class. Draw on information in the case itself, other
readings, your relevant work experience, and any assumptions you may consider reasonable.

1.       What are the key differences in operating strategy and structure between Halloran and Allied?
What impact do those differences have on the kind of businesses they are an the way they want to
operate?
2.       What are the strengths and weaknesses implicit in Allied’s operating stance? Halloran’s? A
priori, how would you expect an economic downturn to affect the two firms? What about an upturn?
3.       What economic risks are implicit in Halloran’s operations choices? How has the firm sought to
reduce these? How successful have they been?
4.       What should Jim Rochleay recommend to the president?

Suggested Complementary Reading:
Strategic Planning - Forward in Reverse?, Robert H. Hayes, Harvard Business Review, November
01, 1985. (Product Number: 85607)

SESSION 6. Operations based advantages and reaction to price wars.
ChemBright, Inc. (Product Number: 9-693-026)

ChemBright is a small start-up company that manufactures private-label household chemicals who
faces a price war with a bigger company from another region.

Be prepared to discuss the following questions in class. Draw on information in the case itself, other
readings, your relevant work experience, and any assumptions you may consider reasonable.

1.    What is your evaluation of ChemBright’s Strategy? How sound is the business at this point?
What challenges could Steve Vitale make to improve the company’s profitability?
2.    What should Steve Vitale do about the price war?
3.    If this threat is surmounted, what avenues of expansion appear most compromising for
ChemBright?

                                  Part II
      VALUE CHAIN DESIGN PLANNING AND EXECUTION UNDER UNCERTAINTY

Day 4
SESSION 7 Agility under uncertainty.

A lecture introducing the concept of operations agility, which is key to reacting and thriving in highly
uncertain environments such as LatAm.

Suggested Complementary Reading

What Is the Right Supply Chain for Your Products? Marshall L. Fisher, Harvard Business Review,
March 01, 1997. (Product Number: 8509).

SESSION 8 An example of an integrated strategy and operation in Manufacturing+Retail, On
Line+Physical.

Zara: An Integrated Store and Online Model (A and B) (Product Number: 620073-PDF-ENG
and 620074-PDF-ENG)
In 2010, amidst the growth of ecommerce and the emergence of new, purely online, fashion players,
Zara launched its first online store, Zara.com. Since then, Zara's online business had grown at a fast
pace. By 2018, 12% of Inditex Group's total sales came from the online channel. Inditex was committed
to the vision of becoming fully-integrated, fully-digital, and fully-sustainable by 2020.

Be prepared to discuss the following questions in class. Use the information presented in the case, other
readings, your relevant professional experience and any assumptions that you may consider reasonable.

   1. What is fast fashion? How can Zara achieve this? What advantages and disadvantages does this
      strategy have?
   2. How does Zara decide what to manufacture in Europe for Europe (its main market), and what to
      bring from distant, off-shore suppliers?
   3. How could stores continue to be relevant in a world with increasing presence of online
      touchpoints?
   4. What should the store portfolio look like in the medium term?
   5. How should Zara use and advance the integrated model going forward?
   6. What other challenges and opportunities would arise with the increase of online sales?

Suggested Supplementary Reading: The Store Is Dead - Long Live the Store, David R. Bell, Santiago
Gallino, Antonio Moreno, MIT Sloan Management Review, Apr 1, 2018. (Product Number:
SMR655-PDF-ENG)

Day 5

SESSIONs 9 & 10
Global Supply Chain Management Simulation V2 (Product Number: 8623-HTM-ENG)
The simulation gives students an opportunity to design and manage the supply chain of a global phone
manufacturer. During the simulation experience, students design the phone product line, forecast
demand, choose a set of suppliers with different costs, lead-times and capacities, and allocate
production among their chosen suppliers.

You will play this simulation in teams, and your team grade will depend on your team´s performance.

Suggested Supplementary Reading: Making Supply Meet Demand in an Uncertain World, Marshall
L. Fisher; Janice H. Hammond; Walter R. Obermeyer; Ananth Raman, Harvard Business Review, May-
June, 1994. (Product Number: 94302-PDF-ENG)

Day 6

SESSION 11 Mid term planning and execution post COVID 19.
A lecture introducing Sales and Operations Planning (S&OP), the key process for mid range planning,
the Control Tower, the dominating methodology for short term planning and execution, and how to
prepare for and react to low probability and high impact events.

Suggested Complementary Reading

Agility in supply chain planning and execution, Santiago Kraiselburd and Roshan Kumar. Chapter
16 in. “Global Logistics and Supply Chain Strategies for the 2020s: Vital Skills for the Next
Generation”, Springer, Switzerland, 1st ed. 2023.

SESSION 12 Preparing for large disruptions.
Renesas Electronics and the Automotive Microcontroller Supply Chain (A) (Product Number:
612071-PDF-ENG)
Be prepared to discuss the following questions in class. Draw on information in the case itself, other
readings, your relevant work experience, and any assumptions you may consider reasonable.

   1. How would you characterize customer demands for Renesas products? Does the automobile
      industry place unique demands on Renesas compared to consumer electronics (e.g., laptops,
      phones)?
   2. Why is Renesas portfolio so fragmented?
   3. How different are the economics of serving Company A vs Company B?
   4. What alternatives should Renesas consider to reduce its vulnerability to future disruptions?

Suggested Complementary Reading
Building Resilient Supply Chains Won't Be Easy. David Simchi-Levi, Edith Simchi Levi. Harvard
Business Review, Jun 23, 2020. (Product Number: H05P7C-PDF-ENG)

                                     Part III
                  INDUSTRY 4.0 PART A. FOCUS ON AGRIBUSINESS

Day 7
SESSION 13 Industry 4.0, Supply Chain 4.0 and its potential in Agribusiness.

A presentation about the coming of the 4th industrial revolution and its potential impact on Agribusiness
in the region.

Suggested Complementary Reading (available after class):

Monitoring and assessing the impact of Supply Chain 4.0 in Latin America: framework,
application to agribusiness, and policy discussions. Santiago Kraiselburd, Luigi M. Laporte and
Reinaldo Fioravanti, Inter- American Development Bank (IDB) discussion Paper, December 2018.

SESSION 14 The great convergence between chemicals, pharmaceuticals and agribusiness.
E.I. DuPont de Nemours and Co. (A) (Product Number: 9-699-037) and (B) (Product Number: 9-
600-051)

Du Pont's chief executive must decide what steps to take as his company expands from commodity and
specialized chemicals into biotechnology.

Be prepared to discuss the following questions in class. Draw on information in the case itself, other
readings, your relevant work experience, and any assumptions you may consider reasonable.

1.     How is du Pont making the transition to the life sciences? What impact do you think this will
have on other parts of the company?
2.     Where does du Pont think that value (in life sciences) lies? How does it intend to capture it?
3.     As Chad Holliday, what do you envision as the company’s strengths and weaknesses as it goes
forward? What should Holliday do next-should he focus on vertical integration, “virtual integration”?
Something else?
4.     How should DuPont think about marketing its non-GMO STS? How does this STS fit with
DuPont’s overall strategy of creating GMO foods?

Suggested Complementary Reading:
The Life Sciences Revolution: A Technical Primer, Gary P. Pisano; Stephanie Oestreich; Clarissa
Ceruti, Harvard Business Publishing, January 28, 2002. (Product Number: 602118)

Transforming Life, Transforming Business: The Life-Science Revolution, Juan Enriquez; Ray A.
Goldberg, Harvard Business Review, March 01, 2000. (Product Number: R00203)

Day 8
SESSION 15 Farming in emerging markets to feed the planet I
Cresud and Argentina (Product Number: 515043-PDF-ENG)

Argentina-based Cresud managed 1 million hectares (2.5 million acres) of land in South America. For
20 years, the publicly traded company's strategy had been to acquire underutilized properties and turn
them into productive farmland for cattle and crops. In 2014, Cresud's CEO wondered if the strategy was
still correct in the face of falling commodity prices, more powerful input companies, and potentially
positive changes in Argentina's political environment.

Be prepared to discuss the following questions in class. Draw on information in the case itself, other
readings, your relevant work experience, and any assumptions you may consider reasonable.

1. Why are so few agricultural companies listed on stock markets?
2. How does Cresud aim to create value? Can it succeed?
3. How would you characterize Cresud's business model? What implications does this suggest for
Cresud's operations?
4. What should Alejandro Elsztain's growth strategy be for Cresud?

Suggested Complementary Reading:

Farmland Investment: a Technical Note, Ray Golbert, Arthur Siegel, Gustavo Herrero and Andrew
Terris. (Product Number: 9-211-022)
Note on Agriculture in Argentina, David E. Bell, Mary Shellman (Product Number: 9-515-069)
Los Grobo: Farming´s future? (Product Number: 511088-PDF-ENG)

This case describes the international expansion plans of the second largest grain producer in Latin
America, Los Grobo. Based in Argentina with US$550 million in annual sales, Los Grobo also
operated in Brazil, Uruguay, and Paraguay--usually with local partners. Los Grobo had an unusual
business model: it did not own land nor farm machinery. Instead, it created a network of partnered
producers and suppliers.

1.Most companies that have placed themselves at the center of a network organization (for example,
Cisco, Dell, and Amazon) are large and powerful, and operate within technologically sophisticated
industries. Los Grobo has none of these characteristics, yet has
succeeded at building a network organization. How has it accomplished this? What were the key steps
it took? What are the key elements of the network?
2. What advantages or capabilities does the network provide Los Grobo that it could not achieve
otherwise?
3. How does Los Grobo control the network without owning or employing its elements?
4. Isn’t a network strategy riskier than an ownership one?
5. How should Los Grobo expand?

SESSION 16 Farming in emerging markets to feed the planet II

The ITC eChoupal Initiative (Product Number: 9-604-016)
Describes the use of Internet technologies to reach Soybean farmers in India and, in particular,
examines a new system called the eChoupal, developed by the Indian conglomerate ITC.
Be prepared to discuss the following questions in class. Draw on information in the case itself, other
readings, your relevant work experience, and any assumptions you may consider reasonable.
1.     What was ITC’s motivation for creating the eChoupal?
2.     What were the old and new physical flows and information flows in the channel?
3.     What principles did ITC employ as it built the newly fashioned supply chain?
4.     What barriers did ITC face in embarking on this project?
5.     How should ITC develop this platform for the future?

DAY 4. Industry 4.0 (2/2): Focus in Agribusiness
                                       Part IV
                     INDUSTRY 4.0 PART B. FOCUS ON BIOTECH

Day 9
SESSION 17 Long range planning in biotech
Genentech-Capacity Planning (Product Number: 606052-PDF-ENG)

Genentech must develop a long-term capacity plan for a major class of new cancer products. Adding to
the complexity and uncertainty is the fact that the lead time for planning, building, and certifying a new
$600 million plus production-scale facility is five years. The case frames the issues that David

Ebersman, the senior vice-president of products operations, and his management team face and outlines
the approach to date.

Be prepared to discuss the following questions in class. Draw on information in the case itself, other
readings, your relevant work experience, and any assumptions you may consider reasonable.

1. What is your evaluation of Genentech’s production capacity requirements given expected demand
    in 2010 and 2015 for Avastin and Genentech’s other products as per Exhibit 3? Does your
    evaluation change if Genentech wants to cover the 85th-percentile level of demand? (See
    spreadsheet posted on course platform)
2. Assuming Genentech decides to proceed with CCP3, what size production lines (tank sizes) would
    you recommend? Why? What criteria should Ebersman use in selecting a location? Why? Should
    Ebersman move forward with CCP3 now? (If not, when?)
3. What recommendations would you make to Ebersman regarding the process he and his team should
    use in deciding how best to meet the demands for Avastin?
4. A contract manufacturing firm has had an unexpected reduction in demand for a drug it produces. It
    is now offering to devote four 10,000 liter lines to the production of Avastin at a price similar to
    Genentech’s existing contract manufacturing agreements. How should Ebersman respond?
For the assignment questions, assume the following:
a) Each of the two contract manufacturers can devote two 10,000 liter tank lines to Genentech
production, and Genentech hopes they will achieve yields similar to those at Genentech’s own plants.
These tanks will be fully utilized in the production of Rituxan and Herceptin.
b) Industry experts make demand forecasts for drugs like Avastin, but a number of sources of
uncertainty—yet to be determined dosage amounts and treatment regimens, unexpected problems in the
FDA’s approval process, unexpected success or failure of a competitor’s product, and unexpectedly
large or small consumer uptake—means that real demand will depart from the experts’ forecasts. For
purposes of our case discussion, assume that future demand is distributed normally, centered on the
experts’ forecasts, and with variation such that one standard deviation is about 25 percent of the
expected demand. For example, if expected demand is 100 kg per year, then 85th percentile demand
(about one standard deviation above the expected demand) would be 125 kg per year.
Suggested Complementary Reading
Aggregate Production Management, Technical Note UVA-OM-1008, Darden (Product Number:
UV3494-PDF-ENG)

SESSION 18 Real options and pre investing in manufacturing processes.
Eli Lilly and Co.: Manufacturing Process Technology Strategy (Product Number: 9-692-056)

The corporate vice president of manufacturing must decide on the next phase of Lilly's strategy for the
early 1990s, as well as to what extent and what role process development will play. Provides data
outlining three different points in the product development process at which manufacturing process
development might be initiated.

Be prepared to discuss the following questions in class. Draw on information in the case itself, other
readings, your relevant work experience, and any assumptions you may consider reasonable.

1. How would you characterize Eli Lilly´s approach to manufacturing before 1987? How much
   progress have they made since then?
2. What recommendations would you make to Joe Cook regarding the three alternatives for process
   development? Why? Please be prepared to support your recommendation in class with the relevant
   quantitative analysis.
3. What steps must be taken to implement your recommendation?

Suggested Complementary Reading:
"Applying ‘options thinking’ to R&D valuation", Faulkner, T.W., Research – Technology
Management, Vol. 39 No.3, pp.50-6, 1996.

Day 10

SESSION 19 Challenges of Biotech startups from the south.

Inmunox

Inmunox is an R&D start-up, created with the goal of developing vaccines and treatments for infectious
and neglected diseases in Latin America, at an affordable price. As their first product, they have created
a proposal for a SARS-CoV-2 second generation vaccine which they plan to finish by 2022.

Be prepared to discuss the following questions in class. Draw on information in the case itself, other
readings, your relevant work experience, and any assumptions you may consider reasonable.

   1. What are the different options for Inmunox? What does it imply to be an R&D start-up for the
       different options available?
   2. What are the different strategic implications in the short and long term of the different options
       for each Phase for Inmunox?
   3. What is the possible market Inmunox could capture with their SARS-CoV-2 vaccine? And how
       many bioreactors would they require if they wanted to manufacture the vaccine until is ready for
       commercialization?
   4. Considering the differences in risk for each stage, perform an Options Thinking analysis to
       establish the investment opportunity of each decision the board of Inmunox is facing. What
       would be your recommendation for Inmunox, and how would you justify it to the board of
       directors?
