BITSoM
2 Year Flagship MBA Program
Course Outline

Course Title: Valuation
Faculty: Sughosh Moharikar
Dates: September 28 to October 11, 2026

Course Objectives
The primary objective of the course is to impart conceptual understanding and apply it into practical frameworks for the students to be able to value firms / businesses and equity. The course focuses on learning core valuation concepts, and aims to provide students with various approaches, methods, techniques and practical tools to value running businesses /companies, equity, projects, etc which will come handy in the work life. While some new theory may be introduced in this course, the emphasis is on the practical application and integration of corporate finance concepts to valuing businesses / companies. We will discuss standalone valuation, valuation in specific situations such as business combinations (valuing synergies), control premia, valuation in the context of Leveraged Buyouts and valuation of options.

The course also familiarizes students with the usage of spreadsheets to build financial models for valuation as also appreciate the linkages between valuation and strategy of firms. This course seeks to complement learnings in other courses such as Corporate Finance, M&A and Investment Banking.

Key Takeaways
The course covers various approaches to valuation and follows a practitioner’s approach to enable the students to put these concepts into practical frameworks. We aim to provide a through grounding in the theory and practice of valuation. We will discuss common pitfalls in valuation that need to be avoided and we will discuss some issues that practitioners often ignore at their own peril. We want the students to fully understand the consequences of shortcuts or broadbrush assumptions that one might make, and appreciate where such shorthand is justified since it bears little on the valuation, and where these could lead to more serious errors.

Learning Goals
By the end of the course the student should be able to:
Understand the various approaches to valuation
Imbibe and be able to apply appropriate valuation methods and techniques depending on the context
Understand and apply the concept of free cash flows
Learn and be able to build a basic financial forecast and a discounted cash flow valuation model
Get an understanding of nuances of DCF valuation (terminal value, logical linkages with the realities of business operations)
Understand relative valuation and its connection with the DCF model
Value companies using several alternative fundamental valuation models such as real options
Apply valuation models in a manner consistent with underlying theoretical constructs
Course Requirements
Knowledge of financial statements analysis, and concepts of corporate finance. Familiarity with corporate strategy, and economics is desirable.  Working knowledge of excel is needed.
Course materials
Session slides and excel sheets would be provided, which would form the basic material for studies. Academic / other papers specific to the topics will be circulated post the corresponding session(s), as required.
Two optional excellent texts are- ‘Investment Valuation: Tools and Techniques for Determining the Value of any Asset’ by Aswath Damodaran (Wiley) and ‘Valuation: Measuring and Managing the Value of Companies’- McKinsey & Co; Tim Koller, Marc Goedhart, David Wessels.

Assessment
The course utilizes readings, exercises, a detailed valuation project, one quiz and a final exam.

1. Class Contribution             10%
2. Quiz closed book               10%
3, Group Exercise 	        10%
3. Group Valuation Project    30%
4. End term closed book        40%

Session Schedule (Could be modified based on student needs and preferences)
Session
Content

1.

Introduction to valuation, different approaches to valuation. Understanding valuation and value creation principles through analogy models.
RoIC - the holy grail of valuation, introduction to economic profit

2.

Getting familiar with the building blocks of DCF - Cost of Capital.
From risk to cost, cost of debt, cost of equity, understanding and computing beta in real situations, putting the CAPM model to work, weighted average cost of capital computation under different capital structures, a checklist for computing cost of capital

3.

Getting familiar with the building blocks of DCF – Free Cash Flow.
Cash flow and earnings, relevance of cash flows, understanding free cash flows, computing FCFF and FCFE, understanding terminal value, understanding Enterprise Value, computing Enterprise Value and Equity Value

4.

Putting DCF to work – forecasting financials, working out an actual valuation model live, convergence of valuation in the same case

5.

Nuances in DFC, Issues encountered in DCF, Good practices for DCF forecasting, tackling taxation issues, putting concepts like perpetuity growth rate to practice, employing concepts of reinvestment rate and growth, the value driver formula for a logical check. Factoring in cash, holdings and cross holdings

6.

Specific Situations

Business combinations - considerations such as synergy, control premium that weigh in for final deal values (modifications to DCF), A case exercise in applying DCF

7.

Specific Situations

The Leveraged Buyout (LBO) and working out an LBO valuation model

8.

Relative Valuation – principles of relative valuation, understanding valuation multiples and the fundamentals driving them, in depth understanding of commonly used multiples like P/E, EV/ EBITDA, P/B, etc. Good practices in using multiples.

Tying DCF and relative valuation together

9 & 10.

Valuing Options and Applications

Introduction to options, put / call options and put / call parity, option prices in one period model, risk neutral probabilities, binomial method and Black Scholes model

Real options valuation applied to a business situation$T5OUTLINE$),
