                                     Course Outline

Course Title: “Corporate Laws- A Business Perspective”

Faculty: Prof. Kishu Daswani

Dates: October 12th to October 25th 2026
Timings- As per attached schedule

Course Objectives:
This course is designed for MBA students who have not had significant exposure
to business laws.
The course will reinforce and review essential elements of corporate and contract laws
in India. It would familiarize the participants with framework of Indian laws and
regulations, principles of interpretation of statutes, formation, performance and
discharge of contracts, certain fundamental doctrines of corporate law, provisions of the
Companies Act 2013 (with rules),the Indian Contract Act, 1872 and the Sale of Goods
Act, 1930.
In addition to an intense study of the abovementioned the laws, the course in particular
will ensure that
1. The students will understand the economic rationale for the design of the IBC and its
implications for the financial market. This module will then take a deep dive into the
provisions of the IBC which deal with the resolution of corporate persons, and discuss
key case-law.

2. The students will examine the SEBI (Substantial Acquisition of Shares and Takeover)
Regulations, 2011, its history and implications, including an insight into the substantive
objectives of the regulations, charging provisions, procedures for conduct of open offers
and consequences of contravention along with a discussion of relevant case law.

3. The students will examine the elements of insider trading including classical and
misappropriation theory, market manipulation through interference in price discovery
mechanism and other securities frauds. New types of offences like front running or
trading violations through algorithmic trading will be discussed. Methods of surveillance,
detection and deterrence will be explored. The concepts of “Chinese Walls”, “Trading
Window”, “Whistle-blower” and “Short Swing Profit” along with policy / mechanism to
prevent possible insider trading or manipulation.

4. The students study the overarching role of the Sale of Goods Act, 1930 in everyday
trade and commerce.

