BITSoM
                           2 Year Flagship MBA Program
                            Course Outline ( draft ver. July 15, 2026)

Course Title: Corporate Innovation & Venture Building (CIVB) [formerly ‘New Venture Creation’]

Faculty: Srikant Sastri

Dates: October’25-January’26

Course Objectives:
Innovation, entrepreneurship and intrapreneurship are now part of mainstream business
discourse. While the booming startup eco-system in India reflects the surge in
entrepreneurship, bigger companies in the corporate sector are also embracing innovation
and intrapreneurship to stay relevant and succeed in a fast-changing world. However,
innovation needs to be rooted in customer-centricity. Many promising innovations have gone
astray because of excessive fixation on technology & valuation, with inadequate attention to
customers, revenues or long-term scalability.
This hands-on bootcamp course will intertwine theory & practice. Students will learn key
concepts of innovation and venture design, with focus on customer-centricity. On the one
hand, students planning for careers in the corporate sector will have the confidence to be
successful intrapreneurs driving innovation in these larger companies (‘The Intrapreneur
Track’). On the other hand, students with entrepreneurial dreams would be well equipped to
pursue that path (‘The Founder Track’). This course also aims to prepare students with the
right balance of hard and soft skills.

Students opting for ‘The Intrapreneur Track’ (‘Corporate Innovation’ track) will experience how
a Pilot(PoC) Design can be built with alignment to a company’s corporate strategy. Those in
‘The Founder Track’ will experience the full journey from a raw idea to a MVP (Minimum Viable
Product). In both cases, the focus will be on creating sustainable, scalable ventures with tech as
key enabler.

Use of AI will be strongly encouraged at all stages during this course. Students may use no-Code
& AI tools to quickly build and test functional MVPs & pilot projects. Gen-AI can be used for
faster market sizing, to pre-test your customer interviews, to finesse the customer personas, and
to stress-test some of your ideas.

Each class session, a ‘hard’ or ‘soft’ startup concept (“theory”) will be introduced in the class.
Students will then (working in groups) execute that concept (“practical”) in the weeks to come.
A simulated ‘game’ will be used across the 13 classes to provide these groups an experiential,
and enhanced view of the progress they are making.

Key Takeaways:
   1. Master the “0 to 1” Lifecycle: Learn to navigate this high-ambiguity phase, a skill
      highly prized by consulting firms & other recruiters, and very valuable for aspiring
      entrepreneurs also
   2. Become well-versed in key concepts like problem identification, ideation,
      customer development, MVP/Pilot, business models, defensibility, and go-to-market
      strategy.
   3. Gain hands-on experience of ‘AI-enabled accelerated innovation’ all the way
      through to creating and testing a MVP or Pilot project.
   4. Leave this course with a portfolio (a 90-sec video, a pitch deck, a prototype) that proves
      you can build from “0 to 1”, and becomes a powerful addition to your CV
   5. Students begin to appreciate the need to take a reflective and well-
      balanced approach to their journey as an intrapreneur or entrepreneur, rather than
      get carried away by hype.
   6. Learn life-skills like teamwork, real-time iterations, overcoming roadblocks, and
      storytelling.

Learning Goals:

   1. To expose students to all the key concepts and steps involved in designing & building a
      new venture, whether as a new unit in a big company, or a start-up
   2. Bridge Strategy & Execution: Demonstrate the ability to move from a high-level
      ‘Problem Statement’ to a tangible ‘MVP’ or ‘Pilot project’ that can deliver results
   3. Provide hands-on experience of AI-accelerated venture development in a
      near-real corporate or startup environment, incl. incorporating customer feedback
   4. Building a Resilient Temperament: Develop the ‘mental muscle’ to navigate
      through the endless cycles of highs and lows in new venture creation
   5. Develop Storytelling & Influence skills: Master the art of pitching ideas to
      stakeholders, whether investors or a corporate board of directors

Course Requirements:

Required Readings: The Storyteller’s Secret (Macmillan); The Ventilator Project (Pan
Macmillan); Stories that Stick – Kindra Hall (Harper Collins); Lean Spark – Jaideep Prabhu,
Mukesh Sud & Priyank Narayan

Team formation: Groups of 4-5 (depending on class size) must comprise a set of people with
complementary skill sets. Each group should ideally have at least one person who is able to
use AI tools to build simple prototypes (e.g. website/app/chatbot), and one person with digital
marketing skills.

Pedagogy:
Classroom sessions will feature a mix of concepts, several case-lets, a simulated ‘game’, and
industry stories. Some classes are likely to feature a case-study and/or guest speaker.
Students will work in groups and submit one group assignment midway through the course.
The final submission will be in four parts – group video, group presentation, group
submission, and an individual submission. Students should expect to spend 2–3 hours of work
outside class each week to ensure rapid progress.
Use of AI will be strongly encouraged at all stages. Students may use no-Code & AI tools to
quickly build and test functional MVPs & pilot projects.

Assessment:

Assessment Criteria: Class Participation (30%); One mid-semester Group Submission (20%);
Final Group Presentation, Video & Submission (40%); Final Individual Submission (10%)

Session Schedule

Session 1
 (1) Dare to Dream – who & what inspires you?; (2) Course blueprint: Innovation & venture
creation landscape (tech-centric; customer-centric; corporate venture innovations); (3)
Personal Innovation Charter (PIC); (4) Choosing the ‘Founder Track’ or ‘Intrapreneur
(Corporate Innovation) Track’; (5) Group formation

Session 2
 (1) Sharpening the group’s Problem Statement; (2) Inspiration for great ideation; turn your
beliefs upside down; (3) Customer Development plan: Voice of Customer (VoC), Hypothesis
Validation, User Personas; (4) Using Gen-AI to simulate customer interviews & pre-test
questions

Session 3
 (1) Market Sizing: top-down & bottom-up methods; (2) Rapid market sizing using AI & Gen-AI
(TAM/SAM/SOM); (3) Corporate Intrapreneurs: using AI to estimate cannibalisation of existing
products; (4) Product/Service Strategy & Design: crafting the Value Proposition rooted in
customer insights

Session 4
(1) Product/Service design: product features & user experience; (2) MVP design (‘Founder
Track’) or Pilot programme design (‘Corporate Innovation Track’); (3) MVP/Pilot execution
plan & Reality Check; (4) Exploring No-Code & Gen-AI tools to build functional MVPs & pilot
projects

Session 5
(1) Why? Why Now? Why Us? – Mid-point exercise where groups defend their Problem
Statement, Idea, customer insights, market sizing, product/service design, and MVP/Pilot

design before a ‘semi-friendly gatekeeper’ (e.g. associate-level VC for Founder Track;
Division Head for Intrapreneur Track);

Session 6
 (1) Business Models & Unit Economics; (2) Moats & Competition – defending the idea
against competitors; (3) Monetisation & Paths to revenue growth; (4) Go-to-Market strategy;
(5) Customer-centric performance metrics

Session 7
 (1) MVP/Pilot stock-taking & Product/Service stress-testing; (2) Teams make first ‘un-
assisted’ attempt to tell their story in a 90-sec video; (3) Classroom peer reviews & critical
feedback; (4) Using Gen-AI ‘Persona Bots’ (CFO, Legal Head, Marketing Director) to stress-
test MVP/Pilot logic

Session 8
(1) Funding strategy (Founder Track: range of funding options; Intrapreneur Track: building
the business case for the corporate board); (2) Storytelling & pitching (Part One) – broad
principles; 10-point framework; logic for the brain; (3) Founder Track: pitching to investors;
Intrapreneur Track: pitching to the company Board

Session 9
 (1) Storytelling & pitching (Part Two) – creating emotion for the heart; infusing X-factor; (2)
Founder Track: connecting the founder’s story to the mission; (3) Intrapreneur Track:
connecting the innovation to the company’s Purpose & Culture; (4) Final Assessment Plan
briefing, incl. format for MVP/Pilot Execution Note

Session 10
 (1) Innovation Showcase – rehearsal & final preparation; (2) Stock-taking: what worked, what
didn’t, and key learnings; (3) Peer feedback on pitch decks & 90-sec videos; (4) Individual
reflection submissions due

Final Assessment
Teams present final 90-sec video, investor/board pitch deck, and group MVP/Pilot Execution
Note. Each team member also submits an individual reflection note detailing their personal
contribution and key learnings.$T5OUTLINE$),
