BITS SCHOOL OF MANAGEMENT
                             Term 5, Block 27, 2025 - 26
                                           Course Outline

Course Title: Advanced Marketing Strategy

Faculty: Prof Wilfred Amaldoss

Dates: 27th October to 9th Nov 2025

Contact details: wilfred.amaldoss@duke.edu

Academic Associate: Anubhuti Kotasthane

Contact details: Anubhuti.Kotasthane@bitsom.edu.in

Office Hours: 5-6pm on class days; By prior Appointment

Course Description:
This course will take the CEO’s perspective on marketing. Specifically, to build the marketing capabilities in you
to address the marketing challenges topmost in the minds of CEOs today in the face of changing marketplace
realities.

If marketing is to be effective in meeting the CEO and Board level challenges, then marketing has to become:
    • more strategic, more cross-functional, and more bottom-line oriented
    • effective in the face of new competitors like retailers, low-cost rivals, and emerging giants
    • help the transition to new channels and new media

Our focus will be on the business unit and its network of suppliers, channels, customer relationships, and
alliances. Taking the viewpoint of the CEO and the general manager, the course will focus on three over-riding
themes:
     • Successful marketing strategies deliver superior customer value
     • Successful implementation of these strategies requires orchestrating the entire value network and
        marketing mix in an effective and consistent manner
     • Marketing innovation can be used as a driver for changing the existing rules of the game in an industry

Key Takeaways:
   • Successful marketing strategies deliver superior customer value
   • Successful implementation of these strategies requires orchestrating the entire value network and
      marketing mix in an effective and consistent manner
   • Marketing innovation can be used as a driver for changing the existing rules of the game in an industry

Learning Goals:
   • Marketing strategy must be integrated with firm strategy, operations, and financial model to be
       successful on a sustained basis.

    •   Understanding customers requires examining customer economics and documenting the value in
        monetary terms.
    •   Retailing is changing, both through the power of the retailer and their private labels as well as the need
        for omni channel solutions demanded by customers
    •   As marketing becomes increasingly digital, marketers need to be more capable of seeing the
        opportunities in multisided markets.
    •   Understanding the role of social influences on purchase decisions and conspicuous consumption

Course Materials: Mentioned session-wise

Assessment:
The general approach is to provoke the participants to think deeply about the challenge of marketing and learn
“how” to think about marketing and consumers rather than simply being told “what” to think. The assessments,
therefore, expand on the course materials and continue the participant’s learning journey, instead of asking for a
repetition of facts, course materials, or what the professor said in class. There are three components to the course
grade:
    1. Class Participation (30%): This part of the grade depends on the quality of your contributions. Highly
         valued contributions include asking insightful questions about the assigned readings building on other’s
         points, redirecting a case discussion when the current point has been adequately covered, providing
         appropriate quantitative analysis, summarizing and/or reconciling previous comments, and drawing
         generic learning points from a particular case.
    2. Group Assignment (30%): Students are expected to form voluntary teams 5-6 students. Your team will
         submit analysis of Medicines Company case (see session 3) and Keurig at Home case (see session 6).
         Details questions on the assignments will be provided in class.
    3. Final Exam (40%): It will be an in-class exam, and it will be related to the broad themes of the class.
         Scheduled: 8th November 2025

