Course Schedule

Module 1: Introduction: The FDE Role
Prof Srinivas Pingali
Key Learning Outcomes
26-10-26 (Mon)

The FDE  Role: Origins, Mission, and Career Trajectory

Articulate FDE value proposition vs. SE, SA, and consultant roles; understand career paths at AI companies
Evolution of customer-facing technical roles and emergence of the FDE model
FDE value proposition versus Solutions Engineer, Solutions Architect, Consultant, and Product Manager roles
Analysis of representative FDE job descriptions from leading AI and technology firms
Core competencies, expectations, and career pathways for FDEs
Day in the life of an FDE

Module 2: Software Development Foundations, PoCs & Vibe Coding  (Prof Sirisha Peyyeti)
Key Learning Outcomes
27-10-26 (Tue)

Software Development Fundamentals: The Anatomy of an Application

From Idea to Demo: PoCs, Pilots, and Production Explained
Understand the core building blocks of a modern application — frontend, backend, APIs, and databases — and how they fit together; confidently explain a simple system to a client in plain language

Difference between a proof-of-concept, a pilot, and a production system; know what “good enough” looks like at each stage; scope a small PoC that shows business value quickly
28-10-26 (Wed)

Designing for Users: UX Basics and User Personas

Building Personas and Design Artifacts with AI
Learn core design and user-experience principles; understand what user personas are and why they help you build the right thing

Use AI tools to create user personas, user journeys, wireframes, and simple mockups; turn a short business brief into design artifacts that guide a PoC
29-10-26 (Thu)

Vibe Coding: Building Working Prototypes with AI Tools
Use AI-assisted “vibe coding” tools (such as Claude Code, Cursor, Replit, v0, and Lovable) to turn a plain-English idea into a working prototype; understand what these tools do well and where to be careful

Module 3: Claude Mastery & Prompt Engineering –
Prof Srinivas Atreya
Key Learning Outcomes
30-10-26 (Fri)

Claude Architecture: Model Family, Capabilities, and Appropriate Use
Select the right Claude model (Opus, Sonnet, Haiku) for a given task; understand context window, speed, cost trade-offs. Capability versus cost versus speed.

Mixing tiers in one system – cheap model triages, expensive models handles the hard cases.

Safety & Reliability posture
Cost estimation for various classes of LLMs.
Future proofing the architecture
02-11-26 (Mon)

Prompt Engineering Fundamentals: Clarity, Structure, and Control

System Prompts and Persona Engineering

Advanced Prompt Patterns: Decomposition, Verification, and Self-Critique

Structured Output Design and Reliability
Write precise zero-shot and few-shot prompts; use XML tags, chain-of-thought, and role-based instructions

Design robust system prompts for enterprise deployments; set behavioural guardrails and persona constraints
Break complex tasks into smaller prompts; build self-checking prompts; add basic safety guardrails to catch mistakes.

Elicit reliable JSON and XML outputs; design output schemas; handle edge cases and hallucination detection

Edge case management
03-11-26 (Tue)

Multimodal Claude: Vision, Documents, and Mixed-Input Workflows

Evaluation Frameworks: Measuring Prompt Quality at Scale
Build pipelines that process images, PDFs, and text; design document analysis workflows for enterprise clients

Working with low quality OCR scans.

LLMs versus structured table models

Design prompt evaluation rubrics; run A/B prompt comparisons; build a repeatable quality gate

Module 4: Agentic AI Systems & Multi-Agent Orchestration- Prof Srinivas Atreya
Key Learning Outcomes
04-11-26 (Wed)

Agentic AI Fundamentals: From Chatbots to Autonomous Systems

Orchestration Patterns: Single Agent, Multi-Agent, and Hierarchical Architectures
Distinguish agentic from non-agentic architectures; understand autonomy, tool use, and loop design

When to avoid agents?

Understand single-agent, sub-agent, and orchestrator–executor patterns; learn to choose the right one for a given business problem

Specialist versus Generalist agents

Planner – worker – Synthesizer pattern
05-11-26 (Thu)

Tool Design for Agents: Principles, Schemas, and Reliability

Human-in-the-Loop Design: When and How to Pause Agents
Design tools that agents use reliably; write precise tool schemas; handle tool failure, timeout, and retry patterns

Idempotency & how to design robust tools for agents

MCP & standard tool interfaces

Identify decision points requiring human approval; design HITL checkpoints; implement interrupt and resume patterns

Progressive autonomy – earning trust approaches
06-11-26 (Fri)

Multi-Agent Coordination: Communication, State, and Conflict Resolution

Agentic System Evaluation: Monitoring, Debugging, and Drift Detection
Design a multi-agent system with shared state; implement agent communication protocols; handle coordination failures

Deadlocks & Livelocks – detecting and breaking them

Instrument agentic workflows for observability; design evals for agent behaviour; detect and respond to behavioural drift

LLMs as Judges – grading traces at scale

Agent observability frameworks

Module 5: Solution Design & System Architecture for Enterprise AI:
Prof Shankar Prakash
Key Learning Outcomes
18-01-27 (Mon)
Architecture Patterns & Platform Landscape
Solution Architecture Sketch for Your Use Case
Identify LLM application patterns (RAG, agents, classifiers, generators, tool-augmented); match patterns to business problem types; briefly compare deployment options (managed API vs VPC/private endpoint vs hybrid) and what GCP/AWS/Azure mean for an AI solution, without cloud ops depth
Translate a business problem into a logical architecture diagram; identify data sources, model layer, retrieval layer, user channel, and integration points; articulate design in stakeholder language
19-01-27 (Tue)
RAG Foundations: Retrieval, Embeddings, Chunking
RAG in Practice: Build Your First Pipeline
Explain RAG end-to-end; distinguish lexical vs semantic retrieval; evaluate chunking strategies; predict failure modes for Indian document types (scanned PDFs, regulatory tables, mixed-format circulars); apply light enterprise cues (metadata filters, entity-aware chunking) without full ontology engineering
Build a working RAG pipeline: ingest documents, embed, retrieve, generate; run test queries; assess retrieval relevance for domain-specific questions; decide what belongs in context vs retrieval vs tools
20-01-27 (Wed)
Production Constraints: Latency, Cost, Reliability
Integrated Solution Design Studio (Working Session Format)
Estimate inference cost at Indian enterprise scale; design for latency SLAs; implement caching, retries, and fallback; express technical trade-offs as business commitments (cost per query, p95 latency); apply a brief architecture-level security checklist (PII touchpoints, residency, injection surface)—full governance and DPDP depth in Module 6
Synthesize pattern, RAG, context, and production choices into one coherent architecture; document and defend design decisions in writing for stakeholders

Module 6: Enterprise Deployment & Responsible AI:
Prof Shankar Prakash
Key Learning Outcomes
21-01-27 (Thu)
Data for Production: Lifecycle, Ownership & Readiness
LLM Evaluation: Frameworks & Evaluation Suite
Map data modalities, sources, sensitivity, and ownership for a production LLM deployment; assess fit for RAG/LLM use using quality dimensions (completeness, accuracy, timeliness, consistency); define lineage and refresh strategy
Distinguish benchmark-based vs judgement-based evaluation; design domain-specific evals and a maturity path from ad hoc to production-grade; build a repeatable eval dataset and scoring approach; run batch evaluation and prioritize improvements
22-01-27 (Fri)
Responsible AI, Bias & Safety in Practice
Production Monitoring & Continuous Improvement
Apply responsible-AI principles to LLM deployments; understand origins of bias (data, prompt, retrieval, model) and basic detection; detect hallucination and unsafe outputs; design guardrails (refusal rules, confidence thresholds, human escalation) and explain decisions in plain language
Design monitoring for drift, hallucination rate, latency, and user feedback; build feedback loops for model/prompt/data updates; distinguish alerting from reporting (pipeline/ops focus; agent debugging covered in Module 4)
23-01-27 (Sat)
AI Governance, Compliance & Model Risk
Deployment Readiness Review
Apply India-relevant frameworks (DPDP Act, RBI guidelines for BFSI, healthcare data norms, sector-specific rules); briefly compare to GDPR and EU AI Act; design an AI governance charter; understand model risk management basics including PII, residency, and prompt-injection controls at deployment scope
Consolidate data, eval, responsible AI, monitoring, and governance into a go/no-go deployment decision; connect technical readiness to business value; optional brief close on adoption/resistance if time permits

Module 7: The FDE Role, Commercial Mindset & Client Engagement : Prof Jyothi Korem
Key Learning Outcomes
25-01-27 (Mon)

Enterprise AI Buying and Adoption

