Assessment:

Assessment Criteria
This course has a modest number of deliverables related to the learning objectives. Course grade will be
computed as follows:

         Case write-ups and presentations (50%)
         Assignments (20%)
         Classroom participation (20%)
         Peer evaluation (10%)

Assessment Guidelines
Case write-ups and presentations (50%)
Each group must turn in three of the four assigned case writeups.
Each case write-up assignment has a list of suggested study questions (Annexure). These are only to provide
guidance. Do NOT answer those questions in a question-answer format.
For case write-ups submitted prior to the class, the instructor recognizes the challenges of responding to the study
questions in advance of the class discussion of each topic. The intent is to motivate the class discussion. Please
do not be concerned by the fact that you may not know the exact answer because there may not be a single
answer. These are normally qualitative issues that reflect the assessments and views of the stakeholders.

The write-ups should be professional, addressed to the major decision-maker in the case (e.g., investment
committee of a VC firm). This means you must be precise and comprehensive. The write-ups should be no
longer than two pages (Times New Roman 12 Font size with 1.5 line spacing, please!) plus a few appendices or
tables as needed. In your case write-ups, do not re- invent the wheel. No detailed background is needed in the
write-up – a few lines should suffice (It is a good assumption to make that the instructor has read the case).
Exercise judgment on what is necessary to make the write-up coherent.

The case write-ups will assess the critical and integrative thinking objective of the course. The assignment must
be submitted online. No late submissions will be accepted.

In addition to the write-ups, the student group will be expected to make at least 1 presentation to the class, lasting
no more than 10 minutes. A presentation schedule will be available soon after the course starts.

Homework assignment (20%)
Each student must submit two group homework assignments. No late submissions will be accepted.

Class participation (20%)
Active class participation is very important for this course. The quality of your comments counts as much as your
participation activity level. Because so much of the learning in this course occurs in the classroom, it is important
that you attend every class. As a common courtesy to other students and the instructor, use of electronics such as
cell phones or laptops is not permitted in the classroom. All students are expected to participate in class
discussions. Students should expect to be called upon. You are required to display your name tent in each class to
facilitate teacher/student interaction.

Peer evaluations (10%)
Since a large part of the course grade depends on group work, each student will evaluate every other group
member individually. Specifically, at the end of the course, you will be evaluated (on a 0-10 scale) by each of
your team members based on your participation and attendance in group meetings, contribution to group
preparation, write-ups, and the overall rating as a team player. Peer evaluations assess how the student can work
in teams. Submission of this evaluation form is mandatory for this course. If you do not receive an evaluation
from any one group member, a score of 5 will be assumed for the missing evaluation

