BITS SCHOOL OF MANAGEMENT
                              Term 5, Block 30, 2025-26
                                            Course Outline

Course Title: Strategic Brand Management

Faculty: Professor Shailendra Pratap Jain

Dates: 15th December – 28th December 2025

Contact details: spjain@uw.edu

Academic Associate: Sayali Korde

Contact details: sayali.korde@bitsom.edu.in

Office Hours: By prior appointment only

Course Description:

Introduction
Brands are key strategic assets for an organization; they constitute a significant proportion of the intangible value
of a firm. In recent years, the intangible value of the firm has become larger than the tangible value for many
firms and especially for service firms. A robust understanding of brands, and how firms can develop and
implement brand strategies helps them to connect with customers in a variety of contexts and cultures within and
across countries. This course is meant to address the need for developing an understanding and appreciation of
the key concepts and approaches for developing and managing brands. It will provide linkages between strategic
marketing and business strategy from a viewpoint of aligning customer value with stakeholder and shareholder
value. Issues such as developing and measuring brand equity that are covered in the course also have linkages
with mergers, acquisitions and takeover decisions.

Objectives
At the end of the course participants will have:
    • Developed an understanding of the meaning of brands, brand associations and brand personality.
    • Have an understanding of a model of brand equity.
    • Developed the ability to manage a brand portfolio and brand touch points.
    • Developed the capacity to link brands and brand equity to strategic directions for the firm.
    • The ability to conceptualize and implement approaches to building a brand along with a brand identity
        and labels.
    • Understood dimensions of a Brand Audit, how to conduct one, and its takeaways.

Pedagogy
The course will use a mix of learning methods including lectures, case discussion, presentations, assignments,
and discussions by participants. For each session, participants are expected to have read the assigned reading
material for the session before they come to the session. Where the session has a case, participants are expected

to have read and analyzed the case individually (as well as in their assigned groups as needed) and submitted the
assignment as indicated. The instructor may call on a participant/group at random to present.

Course Materials: In session-wise schedule

Assessment: Maximum Marks 100

    1.   Group Assignments:                        30% (To be announced in class)
    2.   Brand Authenticity (Individual):          20%
    3.   Class Participation (Individual):         20%
    4.   Brand Audit (Final Group Project):        30%

Students can form groups of size 7-8 for the group-based assessments.

Group Assignments
Several group assignments (to be announced in class) will form a key fulcrum for course learning. Please prepare
these assignments as stated in the handout.

Brand Audit
Throughout the course, we will discuss various Brand measurement ‘tools’ towards gaining an understanding of
the Dimensions of a Brand. Eleven of these have been underlined in the outline. These are: Brand Positioning,
Brand Personality, Brand Associations, Brand Portfolio, Brand Experience, Brand Relationships, Brand
Authenticity, Brand Extensions, Brand Identity, Brand Purpose, and Brand Touch Points. A Brand Audit as
conceptualized in this course will encourage you to use these tools in assessing a brand towards conducting a
Brand Audit. Consider using the Brand Equity Framework as the overarching guiding model in your audit.

Here are the specifics of this final assignment:

1. Pick a set of two competing brands.

2. Identify 5-7 ‘tools’ from the course as ‘dimensions’ of conducting the audit. You are welcome to choose other
tools from the Brand Equity model we will discuss in class.

3. Survey at least one group of stakeholders – employees, channel members, consumers, policy makers, etc.

4. Calibrate each brand on each tool-based metric. You may have to conceptualize a few of these metrics from
scratch based on class discussion. Others have standard metrics readily available which you are encouraged to
search for.

5. You are permitted to use BITSoM approved Gen AI platform to analyze your data from step 3 above. And
use the analysis in your report and recommendations. If you do so, make sure, you clearly state what platform
and prompts you used.

6. For the next steps, identify one brand you’d like to focus on.

7. Conduct a SWOT based only on this information.

8. Make recommendations to strengthen this specific brand. This step is most critical and recommendations
should stem from your findings.

9. Deadline for this project is Friday, December 26, 2025, 8 am (before start of class). Please email the
professor and Sayali a single file containing a description of your approach, which tools you used, methodology,
and recommendations and limitations. This file should be maximum 2 pages of front-end content (minimum 10-
point font, 1-inch margins all around, single space) + no more than 5 exhibits (an exhibit can be more than one
page long if necessary). Make sure that the document is professionally created and formatted.

