--- COURSE OUTLINE AS CIRCULATED ---

BITS SCHOOL OF MANAGEMENT
                                    Term 4, Block 20, 2025 - 26
                                                Course Outline

Course Title: International Marketing

Faculty: Professor Venkatesh (Venky) Shankar

Dates: July 14th to 27th, 2025

Contact details: venky@venkyshankar.com

Academic Associate: Anita Yadav

Contact details: anita.yadav@bitsom.edu.in

Office Hours: By prior appointment only

Course Description:

This course is designed to give you a good understanding of how marketers evaluate, develop, and implement
marketing strategies and programs in the international context, to meet with the needs of their customers while
achieving their business objectives. It will enhance the skills you may have developed in earlier marketing course(s)
by emphasizing their application in an international context. The course is designed for students who expect to
undertake international marketing assignments, work for multinational corporations, or help smaller companies
expand internationally. The focus will be on understanding of international marketing concepts and application of the
concepts in the form of case analysis, discussion of real-world examples, and development and presentation of an
international marketing plan.
The course will emphasize the following key elements:

    1. International Marketing Environment: The focal concern is the impact of external forces that shape
       marketing decisions, i.e., economic, trade, cultural, legal, and political environments.
    2. International Marketing Strategy: The important elements include international market evaluation,
       market entry, and market development strategies.
    3. International Marketing Mix: This section will cover analysis of the key elements of marketing mix
       such as product, price, place, and promotion to make appropriate decisions in the international
       context.

The course will cover the different international regions such as Europe, East Asia, Southeast Asia, Latin America
and North America. It will also cover a gamut of industries ranging from food to software. In addition, the students
can pick an industry and international region of choice for their final project. The major emphasis of the course will
be on the application of the relevant marketing tools to international marketing decisions. Financial analysis of
marketing decisions will be stressed.

Course Delivery and Requirements
To meet the objectives, the course will follow a sequence of readings, lectures, and case analysis. Case analysis will
include class discussion, presentation by guest speaker(s) from industry, and a written case report. A variety of
multimedia, including overhead projector, slide projector, video equipment, and computer will be used.

 Assessment: Maximum Marks 100

     1. Mid-term individual case analysis report 30%
     2. Group project 50%
     3. Class contribution 20%

 Written Case Analysis
 The objective of the individual mid-term written case analysis report is to develop your ability to present your
 analysis of the case and your recommended plan in a written form. You will be provided a take-home case about a
 week before the due date and will be asked to submit a written report of your analysis. Your case analysis report has
 to be clear, concise, and persuasive that your boss (imaginary) should be compelled to adopt your recommendations.
 Your report should address all the discussion questions that will be provided a week prior to the deadline for the
 assignment. It should not exceed 4 pages of written analysis, and an additional 2 pages of appendices. All reports
 must be typed double-spaced in 12-pt. Times Roman font and should not exceed the page limit assigned. The page
 limit does not include the cover page and the table of contents. Further guidelines are provided in Attachment 4.
 You are expected to analyze this case individually and are not supposed to discuss it with others. A good analysis of
 the case requires a good understanding of the concepts discussed until the previous class. However, you are
 encouraged to start working on the case as early as possible. The written case report is due before the start of the
 scheduled class on the day it is due.

 Group Project
 The objectives of the group project are to: (1) develop your analytical skills in a team environment and (2) develop
 your team building and managing skills. Each group should comprise 6 students from the same section. The project
 requires you to work on an international marketing plan of interest to each group. It involves three stages. First, each
 student group will propose a project idea to me. A one-paragraph description of project idea together with the
 members of the group is due the day indicated in the schedule. The project idea should include brief descriptions of
 the proposed product/service offering, the target country(ies), and business viability. Next, after approval of the
 project idea, each group will submit a two-page project proposal outlining the importance, objectives, methodology,
 and time line for the project by the deadline. In the next step, each group will submit its project report with
 completed findings to me before the start of the class on the date listed in the schedule. A suggested outline and
 relevant guidelines for the project report are provided in Attachment 5. The page limit for this assignment is 15
 pages of written analysis, plus appendices. All reports must be typed double-spaced in Times Roman 12-pt. font
 with 1 inch margin on all sides and should not exceed the page limit assigned. The page limit does not include the
 cover page and the table of contents. Your written assignments will be evaluated on the basis of content (70%) and
 presentation (30%). Content will be evaluated on the following dimensions: identification of key issues (14%),
 relevance and appropriateness of concepts applied (14%), depth of analysis (14%), consideration of alternative
 decisions (14%), and rationale for recommended decisions (14%). Your report presentation will be assessed on the
 following dimensions: clarity of presentation (10%), flow of arguments (10%), and structure of the report and

conformity to requirements (10%). All reports must be typed double-spaced and should not exceed the page limits
assigned. The page limits do not include the cover page and the table of contents. The reports are due at the
beginning of class on the day indicated in the schedule.

Class Contribution
Class contribution is a major aspect of the course. Your learning will be greatly enhanced by exchanging and
discussing your ideas and views in the class. Contributions to the class, especially during case discussions, will be
expected. An evaluation of your class contribution will be based on both the quality and the quantity of your
contributions, especially during case discussions. Quality of contributions, however, will receive greater
consideration than quantity of contributions. Class attendance and punctuality, therefore, are very essential. I may
call upon anyone at random to address specific questions raised in the class. If at any time during the course you
wish to discuss my perception of how well you are doing, please talk to me.

If you miss three sessions in any course, you will obtain a letter grade lower than what you would have otherwise
received. If four sessions are missed, you will receive a letter grade that is two levels lower, and if 5 sessions or
more are missed, you will receive an 'F' grade for the course. The following instances will also be treated as
absences unless you obtain the instructor’s permission in advance.
    •   Attending only part of a session, either entering or leaving during a break
    •   Arriving in class after the session is scheduled to begin
    •   Attending class with another section
    •   Failing to display your name card
    •   Sitting outside of the allotted seat

Please remember to take the instructor’s permission in advance for any absences whether excused (extreme illness,
supported by a medical certificate or personal bereavement) or otherwise.

Grading
A relative overall grading system will be used. Your overall grade will be a weighted average of your grades in the
different components. Further details of grading will be provided in class. Late submission of assignments will be
penalized with lower grades. Assignments should be submitted before the start of the class on the due date. The
deadline for submission will be strictly enforced. At the end of the course, a completed peer evaluation form will be
collected from each student to determine if the individual members in a group made equitable contributions to group
efforts (Attachment 6). An individual who contributes less than equal share than her/his peers in her/his group will
be penalized with grades lower than that of her/his group, consistent with the extent of contribution.

Key Common Dates and Deadlines:
Session number  Item
Session 3      Project Teams and Idea Paper Due
Session 5      Project Proposal Due
Session 6      Mid-term Individual Case Report Due
Session 10     Final Project Report Due

Conduct
You are expected to abide by BITSOM’s Code of Academic Integrity, which will be enforced in this course. Any
violations will be strictly dealt with according to BITSOM rules. If you have to miss any class for any genuine
reason, please inform me in advance as far as possible. Please do not come late to any class or leave the class any
time before the end of the class. Please refrain from chatting, reading unrelated materials, eating, drinking, sleeping,
and any other distracting or disruptive behavior during the class. Please turn off your cell phones or pagers or other
communication devices during the class and refrain from using any of these devices.

Continuous Improvement
I am committed to continuous improvement in the quality of teaching and learning. To achieve this, I will use
several mechanisms. First, you are invited to speak to me freely at any time about any aspect of the course. Second,
I will distribute 3’ x 5’ “sun-mud” cards twice during the course. You may indicate the merits and demerits of the
course and your suggestions for improvements on these cards. You need not write your names on the cards. Your
feedback will help me gauge how the course is progressing and make it a worthwhile learning experience for you.
After each feedback administration, I will share the summary of your feedback with you and will try to make the
necessary adjustment in my teaching based on your feedback. Third, I will administer a survey of feedback on
course material through a form (Attachment 7). Fourth, I will seek your input on the time you spent on your course
assignments in another form, to be distributed in the class (Attachment 8). The third and fourth mechanisms will be
particularly helpful for me to make the course more effective for future classes. Finally, course evaluation forms will
be administered at the end.

Special Needs: Any student with special needs should bring this to the attention of the instructor as soon as possible,
but not later than the second session.

