Course Description:

Course Objectives
The principal objective of the course is for the students to develop a fundamental understanding of the mergers
and acquisitions (M&A) phenomenon, and to provide them a foundation which would come in handy in their
work life – whether working in corporate strategy - evaluating and employing M&A strategies for their
businesses, or carrying out business development function through business alliances, or working in specialised
M&A advisor roles (investment bankers, financing providers or management consultants). The emphasis is on
the financial aspects and financial considerations for M&A transactions; however, the course will weave in the
complementary aspects of corporate strategy and the softer aspects for handling M&As.

Key Takeaways
We aim to equip the students with the basic tools and skills to become familiar with, appreciate, and understand
M&A transactions so they can analyse, evaluate and execute M&A transactions more effectively in their work
life. We will provide them with an understanding of the regulatory and financial environment that impacts the
efficiency or doability of the transactions. We will cover the core elements of M&A including the linkage with
strategy, the M&A process, financial analyses, deal structuring, takeover defences etc. with the conceptual
framework and the application in the Indian scenario, which would also include examples of deals in the Indian
context.

Learning Goals
Provide students with a grounding in
1. Why M&As occur, and an understanding of other modes and means available to a corporate leader (like
   alliances/joint ventures, etc.) to achieve similar strategic objectives.
2. How M&As happen – a structured framework and approach to the entire acquisition process; and on the flip
   side - running an efficient sell-side process

3. Impact of the regulatory framework (in the Indian context) in effecting M&As
4. How to value businesses - Understanding and application of different valuation techniques; appreciation of
   the concepts and assumptions underlying corporate valuation
5. Understanding of the mechanics of the popular LBOs and how to model LBOs
6. Understanding and working out merger calculations, accretion / dilution analysis
7. Characteristics of different deal structures; how deal structures impact deal economics and terms
8. Commonly encountered tactics in public market takeovers esp. hostile takeover raids and defences; with a
   particular emphasis on India.

Course Materials:
Required readings- Mentioned session-wise

Optional readings- Mentioned session-wise

Course Requirements
Knowledge of financial statements analysis, and concepts of corporate finance. Familiarity with corporate
strategy, economics, and corporate law (to the extent covered already).

