dates and session numbering may be carried over from a previous cohort — the Term 5
schedule stated above is authoritative. Use the outline for content, syllabus, grading
and policy; use the header above for when and where the course actually runs.

--- COURSE OUTLINE AS CIRCULATED ---

BITS SCHOOL OF MANAGEMENT
                             Term 6, Block 35, 2025 - 26
                                           Course Outline

Course Title: Service Operations

Faculty: Prof Vishal Ahuja

Dates: March 2nd to March 15th, 2026

Contact details: Vishal.Ahuja@bitsom.edu.in

Academic Associate: Vishnu PV

Contact details: vishnu.pv@bitsom.edu.in

Office Hours: By prior appointment only

Course Description:
This course is concerned with the design and delivery of services: how to achieve and sustain service excellence,
how to identify and overcome key challenges in the service delivery, and what methods/metrics to use for
monitoring and analyzing the performance of service entities? Since customers are involved, actively or
passively, in the “production” of services, we strive to understand and leverage the role customers play in the
process. We develop a service framework and model, with the following key components:

•   Service Offering: How to design, improve, and optimize the service offering? What customer needs and
    expectations should the service emphasize and which ones to sacrifice?
•   Firm Value: Does the service provide value for the stakeholders?
•   Employee and Customer Value: Does the service provide value to the employees and customers? What are
    some of the effective ways to engage them? What is the role of digital experience?

The course explores a variety of industries and is designed for students whose interests tend towards the end
consumer: those who plan to work in service-oriented firms/roles, and those who need to understand and
evaluate services from an external perspective, for example, management and technology consultants.

This course draws ideas not only from operations management, but also from consumer behavior, marketing, and
strategy. The course is a mix of softer, more qualitative, frameworks and more analytical pursuits such as process
flow/analysis (with a focus on service processes), waiting line models, lean principles, and revenue management.
Process flows are used to understand the aggregated flow of customers through a process to address issues such
as capacity utilization, throughput, and waiting time of a system. We will discuss Customer Interaction
Diagrams, useful in examining the interactions between the firm and its customers and can help explore service
innovations and the role management should play.
The course is a mix of cases and lectures. Most cases are designed to explore our service framework in a broad
sense and then delve into a particular aspect in some detail. Classroom discussion is integral to the course.

Instruction Methodology: The course is a mix of cases and lectures. Most cases are designed to explore our
service framework in a broad sense and then delve into a particular aspect in some detail.
Class Lectures are devoted to lectures concerning important concepts and issues related to operations
management. These presentations will supplement any readings that are provided for a session.

Case Studies: A sizeable portion of class time will be devoted to the analysis and discussion of case studies.
Students are expected to have read the assigned materials and carefully analyze the cases before coming to class.
It is essential to take an active role in the class discussion. Because the cases assigned are often rich in context,
students are strongly encouraged to work with a study group but are expected to present their own analysis and
perspectives during the class discussions. Students will often be called upon in connection with discussions and
are expected to be able to speak from their analyses. The experiences and background of the student body will
often lead the discussion into new territory, and this is encouraged. However, due to time constraints I may
occasionally feel the need to steer discussion in a manner that ensures that key learning objectives are achieved.
In a typical class, I may ask a few class members (potentially selected at random) to start the session by
addressing a specific question. Anyone who has prepared the case should be able to handle such a lead-off
assignment. As a group we will try to build a complete analysis of the situation and address the issues and
problems represented in the case.
The “detailed course outline” at the end of the syllabus lists the readings, cases, and any homework assignments
for every session. Because class time is our most precious resource, please come to every class prepared.
Essential preparation includes reading the assigned readings and case, doing the assignments, and bringing these
resources and materials to each class.

Course Materials: The presentations for the lectures will be supplemented by class notes as well as technical
and practical readings that will be available through the course website. Material for which I (or BITSoM) hold
the copyright will be distributed through Brightspace, BITSoM’s learning management system (LMS). The
lecture slides will be posted on LMS before the start of each class.

Assessment: Maximum Marks 100

                      DIMENSION                                   GRADE
              Individual assignments: Quizzes (Best 3 of 4)       15%
              Group Assignments: Cases (7)                        25%
              Final project (group)                               20%
              Final Exam (in-class)                               25%
              Class Contribution                                  15%
              Total                                               100%

Individual Assignments – Quizzes (timed, in-class) and Concept Check
   • The purpose of this assignment is to encourage a level of mastery over the analytical tools discussed in
       class. This assignment is to be submitted individually. You are welcome to consult class materials
       ONLY (slides, notes, etc.).
   • There are no make-up quizzes. Note that there are 4 in-class quizzes (that will be timed), but only 3
       will count towards your final grade. Your lowest score is dropped.

Group Assignments
   • Each group should consist of 4-5 people. I also suggest that you make the backgrounds of your group
      members diverse. Please make sure to form groups by the end of the first class.
   • You may not work alone on group assignments and only put your name on the group write-up if you
      contributed. You may not collaborate with other groups in writing the group assignments.
   • Questions for each case/assignment are typically contained within the syllabus. Prepare all questions for
      class discussion.
   • Limit your write-up to less than 1 page: single-spaced, 11 pt Times New Roman, 1-inch margins
   • ONLY turn in written responses via LMS to all questions marked with a                 .
   • Submit ONE assignment per group – as a PDF or Word document. The write-up should be concise and
      include the necessary calculations (if asked) of costs, benefits, or effects. Your answer to the assigned
      questions should be drawn directly from that analysis and should display solid justification for any
      decision that you recommend. If there is a page limit, I will specify in the instructions.

Final Project (group-based)
   • Each student group will partner with a real business and provide a thorough analysis of the service model
       and operational decision/challenge/dilemma faced by the business using concepts and tools learnt during
       the course (for example, CI-Diagram and Process Flow analysis).
   • Each student group is expected to finalize the project by Class 6. The completed project assignment is
       due three hours before Class 10. Further details related to the project assignment will be made available
       as the course progresses.

Peer Evaluation: At the end of the term, I will ask each student to complete a peer evaluation form where they
rate their fellow group members. I will use this to adjust Group grades. The purpose is to identify freeloaders, not
to make minute adjustments of group assignment grades.

Instructions for Assignment Submission:
• Each assignment (group, individual, project) is to be submitted online via the LMS System. All homework
    assignments are due before the class (see LMS for deadlines). Late submission of homework is not
    acceptable and will receive a grade of zero since solutions may be discussed in class.
• Turn in all assignments by following the “Assignment” tab, where you will find a link to upload each
    completed assignment. Make sure to click the SUBMIT button.

The purpose of homework is to facilitate your learning and develop problem-solving skills. In my experience, a
student’s performance in the course is highly correlated with his/her effort in solving the assignments. A
substantial portion of the credit will be based on evidence of effort. Plagiarism will not be tolerated, and effort
will be rewarded.

Expectations/Class contribution:
• The purpose of evaluating class contribution is to encourage a richer learning environment where the source
   of knowledge grows from beyond the professor and course materials to include the experience, intuition, and
   knowledge of the participants.
• A “good” in-class contribution:

         o builds on others’ contributions and the assigned readings,
         o shares personal experience,
         o uses detailed case facts (when appropriate),
         o summarizes and clarifies several previous contributions, and
         o expresses doubts, concerns, limits of the framework or model.
•   You are expected to attend every class and to be well prepared. You will be able to make class contributions
    if you have worked hard on the cases and therefore come to class prepared. It will be impossible to receive
    an “A” in this course without excellent class contribution.
•   You should find a seat you are comfortable with and stay there throughout the course. This will help me in
    correctly assessing student contribution.
•   Use your name card in every class. This is to benefit both the instructor and other students.
•   You will be able to make class contributions if you have worked hard on the cases and therefore come to
    class prepared.

Generative AI Guidelines:
You are welcome to use generative AI to help with your final project. You are responsible for what you say in
class, and its accuracy and reasoning. Similarly, you are responsible for what you write in your final project
write-up, and the accuracy and reasoning therein.

